---
/* THE CARD — the top layer of /pilots/, and only the top layer.

   The brief asks for "Cards first. Detail on click or a short dedicated
   block," and it caps a pilot card at problem / approach / goal, one line
   each. Every earlier round read that cap as a cap on the PAGE, and built
   four two-viewport objects that between them told a funder twenty-five
   words. The cap is here, on the card, where it belongs; the detail it was
   always the summary OF is in pilots-detail.astro, below.

   So this is a card and behaves like one: it is a cell in a four-up
   comparison grid, not a scene. Four of them fit on one screen, which is
   the only way a reader can hold "which of these four" in their head at
   once — the thing the old page made impossible by spending two viewports
   per pilot. No photograph, no display rung above --step-sub, no motion
   beyond the hairline: the picture and the argument are downstairs.

   The anatomy, identical on all four:

     rail    numeral · sector ............................ status
     who     the partner
     name    the title, one serif rung
     tag     one line
     problem one line, on its own shared row
     approach                    "
     goal                        "
     door    the anchor to this pilot's detail block

   The only variable is the numeral. Four institutions making the same kind
   of commitment should look like four of the same thing.

   ── WAVE 5: THE ROWS ARE THE COMPARISON ─────────────────────────────

   Three things were merely fine here and are now exact.

   1. THE FIELDS DID NOT SHARE ROWS. The outer six rows were subgridded and
      locked to the pixel, but PROBLEM / APPROACH / GOAL lived inside a `dl`
      that laid itself out per card. Measured at 1440: the four APPROACH
      labels sat at y 558.0 / 565.6 / 565.6 / 550.4 and the four GOALs at
      628.6 / 643.9 / 643.9 / 636.2 — a 15px spread on both. Two causes, and
      both are gone:
        · a three-line PROBLEM (03) pushed its own APPROACH down while a
          two-line PROBLEM (01) did not, and
        · the `dl` was a grid of auto rows inside a subgrid track sized to
          the TALLEST card, so `align-content: normal` silently stretched
          each card's rows by a different amount — which is why four
          identical "PROBLEM" labels measured 19.3 / 23.1 / 11.5 / 15.3px
          tall.
      The subgrid chain now runs all the way down: page grid → cell → card →
      field list. Every card's PROBLEM begins on one line, every card's GOAL
      begins on one line, and a funder can read across.
      Because a subgrid's own padding would inset its tracks and undo the
      alignment it exists to get, the rule and the air above it are carried
      by the first field row and by the tagline's bottom margin instead.

   2. THE COLUMN WAS SHOVED LEFT. `padding-right` took 34.5px off every
      cell, so the space between a vertical rule and the text on its left
      was 53.2px while the space to the text on its right was 18.7px —
      1 : 2.85 where the eye reads a table column as centred in its bay. It
      also meant the brass hover hairline, whose whole job is to wipe along
      this column's rule, was 34.5px shorter than the rule. Both fixed by
      letting the column be the cell: even bearings, and the last column now
      ends flush with the shell, on the same line as the headline above it.

   3. THE STATUS WRAPPED. "· Proposed" rode the partner name, and at
      "Secretary Miguel Cardona" it wrapped to a second line — so 04's title
      had 42px of air above it where 01–03 had 59px. The qualifier is not
      part of the name, it is the state of the pilot, so it now sits where a
      compare column puts a state: the right end of the rail, opposite the
      numeral. One line of partner in every card, identical air under it.
      The claim is unchanged and still on every card. */
const { pilot: p } = Astro.props;
---
<article class="card">
  {/* numeral · sector, then the state of the pilot. Two of the four
       partners are living, identifiable public officials, and a bare name
       in a partner slot asserts a partnership; nobody has signed one. The
       qualifier rides every appearance of these four on this site — here
       it rides the card rather than the name, which is both truer (the
       PILOT, not the partnership, that carries a stage) and the only way it
       stops wrapping. The word is `p.status` — the prospectus's own, Active
       for 01-03 and Exploring for 04. It used to be the literal "Proposed"
       on all four, which the prospectus contradicts. */}
  <p class="card__rail">
    <span class="index card__i">{p.index}</span>
    <span class="label card__field">{p.field}</span>
    <span class="label card__q">{p.status}</span>
  </p>

  <p class="card__who"><span class="eyebrow card__partner">{p.partner}</span></p>

  <h3 class="display display--sub card__t">
    <a class="card__a" href={`#${p.id}`}>{p.title}</a>
  </h3>
  <p class="card__tag">{p.tagline}</p>

  <dl class="card__f">
    <div class="card__row"><dt class="label">Problem</dt><dd>{p.problem}</dd></div>
    <div class="card__row"><dt class="label">Approach</dt><dd>{p.approach}</dd></div>
    <div class="card__row"><dt class="label label--brass">Goal</dt><dd class="card__goal">{p.goal}</dd></div>
  </dl>

  <p class="card__door" aria-hidden="true">
    <span>Detail</span>
    <svg class="arrow card__arrow" viewBox="0 0 22 10">
      <path d="M16 1l5 4-5 4M21 5H0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" fill="none"/>
    </svg>
  </p>
</article>

<style>
  /* A cell, not a plate. The grid's own hairlines draw the four; giving
     each one edges of its own would make the comparison read as four
     objects again, which is the thing this layer exists to undo. */
  .card {
    position: relative;
    /* the eight rows are the PAGE's rows, inherited through the cell: every
       card's PROBLEM sits on the same line as every other card's, and so
       does every card's APPROACH and GOAL. The block padding is therefore
       carried as margins on the first and last rows — padding on a subgrid
       item would shift its rows out of the shared track and undo the
       alignment it is here to get. */
    grid-row: 1 / -1;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: subgrid;
    row-gap: 0;
    /* no trailing padding: the column IS the cell, so the bearing on each
       side of every vertical rule is the same half-gutter, and the brass
       hover hairline covers exactly the rule it wipes along. */
  }

  /* The brass hairline that wipes along this column's stretch of the band
     rule — the same gesture the homepage register uses, so the two card
     layers rhyme. --bay-l / --bay-r are handed down by the grid: where the
     band's rule is continuous the wipe runs the full bay, ending ON the
     vertical rules either side rather than floating between them, and it
     stops flush with the rule's own ends at the two outer columns. Default
     0, so a card used anywhere else just lights its own width. */
  .card::before {
    content: ''; position: absolute; top: -1px; height: 1px;
    left: calc(var(--bay-l, 0px) * -1);
    right: calc(var(--bay-r, 0px) * -1);
    background: var(--brass);
    transform: scaleX(0); transform-origin: right;
    transition: transform 620ms var(--ease-out);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* Every row is placed by hand. Auto-placement around an explicitly-placed
     three-row field list is correct per spec but not obvious to the next
     reader, and the whole point of this component is that the eight rows
     are a fixed, shared armature. */
  .card__rail { grid-row: 1; }
  .card__who  { grid-row: 2; }
  .card__t    { grid-row: 3; }
  .card__tag  { grid-row: 4; }
  .card__f    { grid-row: 5 / 8; }
  .card__door { grid-row: 8; }

  .card__rail {
    margin: clamp(1.4rem, 2.4vw, 2rem) 0 0;
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .card__field { white-space: nowrap; }
  /* a separator, not a word: --mark is the non-text register */
  .card__field::before {
    content: '\00b7'; margin: 0 0.9ch; color: var(--mark);
    letter-spacing: 0; font-weight: 500;
  }
  .card__q {
    margin-left: auto; padding-left: 1.2ch;
    white-space: nowrap;
    transition: color var(--dur-hover) var(--ease-out);
  }
  /* No opacity on top of --fg-meta. The token IS the bottom of the
     contrast ladder (cream 54%, measured 5.3 : 1); multiplying it by a
     further 0.72 lands 38.9% effective = 3.27 : 1, under the 4.5 : 1 AA
     floor for text this size. */

  /* The line box is set here, not left to the paragraph's inherited strut.
     A <p> at body size holding an 11.7px inline span builds a 25.6px strut
     and centres the label in it, which put ~9px of invisible leading above
     and below the one line on this row — air nobody chose, on the row that
     sets the distance between the numeral and the title. Own the box. */
  .card__who {
    margin: clamp(1.1rem, 2.1vw, 1.6rem) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--step-micro);
    line-height: 1;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .card__partner { transition: color var(--dur-hover) var(--ease-out); }

  .card__t {
    margin: clamp(0.95rem, 1.85vw, 1.5rem) 0 0;
    line-height: 1.06;
    letter-spacing: -0.014em;
  }
  /* the whole cell is the target; the name carries the accessible name.
     The padding is a tap target, not space: a 22px line box is under the
     24px floor the audit holds mobile links to, and the negative margin
     gives the row back so the type does not move. */
  .card__a {
    color: inherit; text-decoration: none;
    display: inline-block;
    padding-block: 0.32rem; margin-block: -0.32rem;
  }
  .card__a::after { content: ''; position: absolute; inset: 0; }
  .card__a:focus-visible { outline: none; }
  /* The ring moves from the name to the whole cell, but it stays the HOUSE
     ring: 1.5px brass, the same weight base.css draws on every other target
     on the site. Only the offset changes, because it is going round a cell
     rather than round a word. */
  .card:has(.card__a:focus-visible) { outline: 1.5px solid var(--brass); outline-offset: 8px; }

  .card__tag {
    /* the bottom margin is the air above the field rule. It lives here, on
       an ordinary item in row 4, because the field list below is a subgrid
       and cannot carry margin without dragging its tracks with it. */
    margin: 0.6rem 0 clamp(1.15rem, 2.2vw, 1.7rem);
    max-width: 34ch;
    color: var(--fg-mute);
    font-size: var(--step-body);
    line-height: 1.5;
    /* balance, not pretty: at a quarter of the page these are two-line
       sentences, and `pretty` only guards the last line — it let 01 break
       inside "follow-up", mid-hyphen, which is a legal break point and an
       ugly one. Balancing splits them at the sense instead. */
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* PROBLEM · APPROACH · GOAL, stacked, one line each, and — the point of
     the layer — one shared row each across all four cards. Stacked and not
     columned: at a quarter of the page's width a two-up setup wraps every
     field to three lines and the "one line each" the brief asks for stops
     being visible as a discipline. */
  .card__f {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: subgrid;
    row-gap: 0;
  }
  /* start, never stretch: a row track is sized by the tallest card in it,
     and a stretched auto row is exactly how the labels drifted before */
  .card__row {
    align-self: start;
    display: grid; gap: 0.3rem;
  }
  .card__row:first-child {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-soft);
    padding-top: clamp(1rem, 1.8vw, 1.4rem);
  }
  .card__row + .card__row { margin-top: clamp(0.7rem, 1.3vw, 1rem); }
  .card__f dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--fg-mute);
    font-size: var(--step-body);
    line-height: 1.45;
    max-width: 38ch;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  /* the outcome is the one line a funder is being asked to believe */
  .card__goal { color: var(--fg); }

  .card__door {
    margin: clamp(1.2rem, 2.2vw, 1.7rem) 0 clamp(1.3rem, 2.2vw, 1.8rem);
    align-self: end;
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75ch;
    font-size: var(--step-micro);
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--fg-meta);
    transition: color var(--dur-hover) var(--ease-out);
  }
  /* The arrow is always drawn. It used to fade in on hover, which meant a
     touch screen — where there is no hover — got a card whose only mark of
     being a door never appeared, and a desktop reader got four cards that
     looked like dead ends until the pointer arrived. The state that changes
     on hover is the advance and the brightening, not the existence. */
  .card__arrow {
    width: 1.7em; color: var(--brass);
    opacity: 0.55; transform: translateX(-5px);
    transition: opacity var(--dur-hover) var(--ease-out), transform 520ms var(--ease-out);
  }

  @media (hover: hover) {
    .card:hover::before { transform: scaleX(1); transform-origin: left; }
    .card:hover .card__partner { color: var(--brass); }
    .card:hover .card__q { color: var(--fg-mute); }
    .card:hover .card__door { color: var(--fg); }
    .card:hover .card__arrow { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  }
  .card:focus-within::before { transform: scaleX(1); transform-origin: left; }
  .card:focus-within .card__arrow { opacity: 1; transform: none; }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .card::before, .card__arrow, .card__door,
    .card__partner, .card__q { transition-duration: 1ms; }
    /* The door lights, the arrow does not slide. */
    .card__arrow { transform: none; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .card__tag, .card__f dd { max-width: 44ch; }
    /* One card at a time, so there is no row to read across and the three
       fields have to separate themselves. The fluid gap bottoms out at
       11px here, which packs label / line / label / line into one grey
       block; the field is the unit, so the gap between fields has to be
       clearly larger than the gap inside one. */
    .card__row + .card__row { margin-top: 1.05rem; }
  }
</style>
